test
```

For local development:

```env
NODE_ENV=development
```

---

# 6. SERVER_MODE

The application supports different server/network configurations:

```text
local
same-server
separate
```

### local

Used when running the application locally.

```env
SERVER_MODE=local
```

### same-server

Used when the backend and frontend are deployed on the same server or domain.

```env
SERVER_MODE=same-server
```

### separate

Used when the backend and frontend are deployed on separate domains.

```env
SERVER_MODE=separate
```

---

# 7. Run MongoDB

MongoDB can be run locally or using Docker.

## Option 1: Local MongoDB

Start MongoDB locally and make sure it is available at:

```text
mongodb://localhost:27017
```

The application uses the:

```text
chat
```

database.

The environment variable should then be:

```env
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/chat
```

---

## Option 2: Docker

The project can run MongoDB using Docker Compose.

Start the services:

```bash
docker compose up
```

When the backend and MongoDB are running inside Docker, the backend connects to MongoDB using the Docker service name:

```env
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://mongodb:27017/chat
```

The hostname `mongodb` refers to the MongoDB service defined in Docker Compose.

Do not use:

```env
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/chat
```

from inside the backend container because `localhost` refers to the backend container itself.

---

